course: biology
lesson: 250
title: Plants deeper: roots and how they drink, hold and push
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: When you pull up a weed and a long white root slides out of the soil, you are holding the plant's anchor, its drinking straw and sometimes its larder all in one.
explain: Roots hold the plant in the ground and take in water and minerals, growing down with gravity and towards water.
explain: Grow a bean in a glass jar and you will see fine white fuzz near the root tip: these root hairs give the root a huge surface for drinking, like a towel with lots of loops soaking up more water than a smooth cloth.
explain: Roots are slow but strong: they grow cell by cell, each cell swelling with water like a tiny balloon, and together they can lift a paving stone.
explain: Not everything under the ground is a root, though: a potato is a swollen stem, and its eyes are buds.
fact: Roots hold a plant in the ground and take in water and minerals; they grow down with gravity, towards water and away from light, and the roots of one rye plant, laid end to end, were measured at over 600 kilometres.
fact: Root hairs are tiny tubes near the root tip that greatly increase the surface for taking in water; each lives only a few days, and new ones keep growing near the tip.
fact: A carrot is a swollen root that stores food so the plant can flower in its second year, so gardeners pick it in the first year; wild carrots are white and thin, and orange carrots were bred by farmers, many in the Netherlands.
fact: Growing roots push slowly but very strongly, because water pressure inside many cells pushes like small balloons, and they can crack paving; most tree roots are in the top metre of soil and spread wider than the branches.
fact: Mangroves grow in salty mud with little oxygen, so some of their roots stick up like snorkels to get air; mangrove roots protect coasts from storm waves and are nurseries for young fish.
fact: A strangler fig starts as a seed high in another tree and sends roots down that wrap round it, sometimes leaving a hollow tower of roots; at Ta Prohm in Angkor, Cambodia, giant tree roots grip the old temple walls.
fact: A plant on a windowsill bends towards the light because a chemical messenger moves to the shady side of the shoot and makes the cells there grow longer; Darwin and his son experimented on this with grass seedlings in the 1880s.
fact: Clover, peas and beans have little lumps on their roots full of helpful bacteria that take nitrogen from the air and turn it into plant food, and the plant gives them sugar; farmers plant clover or beans to make the soil richer.
fact: Many desert plants, like cactuses, have shallow roots that spread wide to catch rain before it dries, while others grow very deep, and a mesquite root was reported over 50 metres deep.
fact: A potato is not a root but a swollen underground stem called a tuber, its eyes are buds that grow new shoots, and green parts of a potato are poisonous and should not be eaten.
fact: Dandelions have deep taproots that store food, a piece of root left in the ground can grow a whole new plant, and roasted dandelion roots were used to make a drink like coffee.
exercise: What are the two main jobs of roots? | Holding the plant and taking in water and minerals | Making the seeds and flowers | Catching sunlight and making sugar from air and water | Breathing out oxygen and making smells
exercise: Which way do roots grow? | Down with gravity and towards water | Up towards the sun | Always sideways along the surface | In circles round the stem
exercise: What is the white fuzz on a bean root in a jar? | Root hairs that soak up water | Mould growing on the bean | Spider silk from the jar | Salt from the water
exercise: Why do root hairs help a plant? | They give much more surface to take in water | They keep the root warm on cold nights in the winter | They scare away worms | They hold seeds for later
exercise: How long does one root hair live? | Only a few days | About a hundred years | Exactly one year | Only a few seconds
exercise: Why does a carrot plant store food in its root? | To flower in its second year | To feed rabbits in winter | To make the soil sweet | To grow back if it is eaten
exercise: What were wild carrots like? | White and thin | Orange and very fat | Purple and round | Green and spiky
exercise: How can a growing root lift a paving stone? | Water pressure in many swelling cells pushes very strongly | Roots shoot up quickly like springs | Roots dissolve the stone with acid only, without pushing at all | Worms help the root to push
exercise: Where are most tree roots? | In the top metre of soil, spreading wider than the branches | Straight down into the ground, about as deep as the tree is tall | Only right under the trunk | Above the ground on the bark
exercise: Why do mangrove roots stick up out of the mud like snorkels? | The salty mud has little oxygen, so the roots get air from above | To catch fish for the tree | To keep crabs off the trunk | To drink up the rain before it ever lands on the salty mud below
exercise: How does a strangler fig start its life? | As a seed high up in another tree | As a cutting planted by people | As a root from under the sea | As a seed buried deep in the soil
exercise: Why does a plant on a windowsill bend towards the light? | Cells on the shady side of the shoot grow longer | The plant is pulled towards the sun by the sun's own gravity | The leaves get heavy on the sunny side | The pot slowly tips over
exercise: What lives in the little lumps on clover roots? | Bacteria that make nitrogen plant food | Tiny eggs that beetles have laid | Drops of honey made by the roots for ants | Baby worms
exercise: Why do many cactuses have shallow roots that spread wide? | To catch rain quickly before it dries up | Because the soil is too soft to hold deep roots | To stop camels pulling them up | Because they need no water
exercise: What is a potato? | A swollen underground stem called a tuber | A swollen root like a carrot | A seed of the potato flower | A kind of fruit that grows under the soil
exercise: What are the eyes on a potato? | Buds that grow into new shoots | Holes made by worms | Spots of mould | Seeds ready to fall out
exercise: Why do dandelions grow back when you pull them up? | A piece of the root left behind can grow a new plant | The flower drops new seeds into the hole you have made | The stem was not pulled out | Dandelions never die
test: In a cartoon a rabbit picks carrots off a tree like apples. What is right? | A carrot is a root that grows under the soil | Carrots grow on trees in the summer | Carrots are the seeds of a flower | Carrots are underground stems like potatoes
test: «mum's potatoes have got shoots and green bits, just cook em» What is right? | Do not eat the green parts, they are poisonous | Green potatoes are the healthiest kind, full of vitamins | The shoots are the tastiest part | Green just means they are ripe
test: «nan says potatoes are roots, same as carrots» What is right? | No, a potato is a swollen stem, a carrot is a root | Yes, both are roots that grow in the soil | No, both are fruits | Yes, but potatoes are older roots that have grown fat and round
test: «3 carrots for each of 4 rabbits, how many carrots» | 12 | 7 | 9 | 16
test: «pulled 6 dandelions, no wait 8, and 3 roots snapped. how many might grow back» | 3 | 8 | 5 | 11
test: What would happen if a farmer planted beans in a poor field this year? | The soil would get more nitrogen for next year's crops | The soil would turn to sand | Nothing would grow there ever again | The beans would take all the water for years
test: «that tree's wrecked the pavement, how?? it's just wood» What is right? | Its roots grew cell by cell and pushed slowly but very strongly | The tree fell over on it in a storm | Someone dug up the stones | Rain soaking in under the stones lifted them up from below, a little each year
test: You turn a pot plant round on the windowsill so the plant leans away from the window. What happens over the next days? | It bends back towards the light | It falls over in the pot and dies | It keeps leaning away from the light | It grows straight down into the soil
test: «my mate says roots just hold the plant up, the leaves drink the water» What is right? | Roots take in water and minerals as well as holding the plant | Yes, the leaves drink all of the water the plant needs from the rain | Roots only store food | Plants do not need water
test: A big wave hits a coast with mangroves, and another with no mangroves. Which coast is better protected? | The one with mangroves | The one without mangroves | Both of them exactly the same | Neither, mangroves make waves bigger
test: Why did people roast dandelion roots long ago? | To make a drink like coffee | To make a strong glue for wooden tools | To feed their cooking fires at night | To make red paint for their faces
test: The roots of a single rye plant were measured end to end. About how long were they? | Over 600 kilometres | Only about 6 centimetres | About 60 metres in all | Exactly as tall as the plant
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the principal functions of a root? | It anchors the plant and absorbs water together with dissolved minerals | It manufactures food from sunlight in the manner of a leaf | It produces the seeds by which the plant is propagated | It serves solely to store food for the following year
exam: Identify the principal advantage conferred upon a plant by its root hairs. | A greatly enlarged surface for the absorption of water | Protection of the root against burrowing animals | The production of seeds beneath the soil | The conduction of sunlight to the root tip
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the potato. | It is a swollen underground stem, or tuber, whose eyes are buds | It is a swollen root identical in kind to the carrot, though rounder in shape | It is the fruit borne by the potato flower | It is a seed which germinates beneath the soil
exam: Ascertain the reason for which farmers plant clover or beans in order to improve their fields. | Bacteria in the root nodules furnish the soil with nitrogen drawn from the air | The plants draw all the stones out of the soil | The roots render the soil impervious to rain | The leaves poison the weeds of the following year, so that the field remains clear of them
